From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Paiva <felipe.paiva@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:55:11 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] platform-ws

WonderSwan and WonderSwan Color get their own shelf, Games/WS, for .ws
and .wsc files on Beetle Cygne (mednafen_wswan, option prefix wswan)
alone, with every platform policy answered explicitly: L, R, X and Y
reach the game, SELECT+X flips the picture, the triggers stay slot's
rewind and fast-forward, rewind is recorded, no crop and no picker. Its
own Screen section has a Shader row (Off, LCD3x, then the retro list,
in Config/ws_shader.txt) and a Picture row (Actual by default, then
Stretch). The title is the file stem, since WonderSwan ROMs carry none.

The footer's last 16 bytes give the colour flag, the save type and the
vertical flag; a file under 64 KB has none, lists, and is sent back out
when the core refuses it. The canvas is now chosen per game: a
horizontal cart draws on the GBA-sized 240x160 canvas and a vertical
one on 360x240, from the footer, read once when the cart goes in.
Every other system keeps the canvas of its core.

Saves need no new mechanism: Cygne hands slot the cart's SRAM or
EEPROM as save RAM and writes no file of its own, so the .sav goes to
Saves/WS/<stem>.sav on autosave, eject and flush, and a cart with no
save type writes none. What the .sav does not hold: no RTC (the core
exposes none; RTC carts keep their clock in states and read the host
clock at boot), and not the console's internal EEPROM owner profile,
which the core rebuilds from options at every boot. A WonderWitch image
persists its SRAM and 512 KB of flash as one block through the same
path, untested.

The shelf shape follows nelynes/slotPlus
(https://github.com/nelynes/slotPlus/commit/273ded9, GPL-3.0). The
shelf shows a placeholder GBA shape until the WonderSwan carts land.
